# Line endings
#
# This repo is edited from two places: a Linux session that writes LF, and
# Windows via GitHub Desktop, where core.autocrlf=true converts to CRLF on
# checkout. `text=auto` alone normalises the *repo* to LF but leaves the
# working tree converted, which is what raises the "this file uses LF line
# endings, but Git is configured to convert them to CRLF" notice on every
# file written from the Linux side.
#
# Pinning eol=lf on the source types makes both sides identical, so the
# notice stops and a file never round-trips through a whole-file diff just
# because it was last touched on the other machine. Every format here is
# LF-safe on Windows — browsers, node, python and VS Code all read LF fine.

* text=auto

*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Windows-native tooling still expects CRLF in these.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Never touch the Nokia source files — a single byte of line-ending
# translation would corrupt them.
*.xlsx  binary
*.xlsm  binary
*.pptx  binary
*.docx  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip   binary
